// Arkansas income tax withholding: "Withholding Tax Formula Method",
// effective 01/01/2026 (Tax Year 2026):
//   1. annual gross pay = period pay x periods (daily = 260)
//   2. net taxable income = annual - $2,470 standard deduction; below
//      $100,001 "look up the income at the $50 range (midrange of $100)"
//   3. annual gross tax = income x rate - bracket adjustment, rounded
//   4-5. minus $29 per AR4EC exemption
//   6. divide by the pay periods
// The bracket table (with its $100-step adjustment phase-out near the top)
// is generated by tools/state/generate_ar.py from the formula PDF. The
// formula's example (Gary: $2,127 monthly, 2 exemptions -> $36.50) is in
// the tests.
//
// Rate: the formula's top rate is 3.70%. The DFA's 2026 employer
// instructions booklet still says "The current withholding tax rate is
// 3.9%"; the formula (the computation method) is followed here and the
// conflict is flagged.
//
// AR4EC input: { exemptions, additionalWithholdingCents?, lowIncome? }.
// Line 5 "I qualify for the low-income tax rates" needs the low-income
// tables, which aren't sourced here, so it throws.
// No AR4EC on file: "your employer must withhold state income tax from
// your wages without exemptions or dependents" (AR4EC).
//
// Wages: federal income-tax wages; the DFA instructions list IRC 401(k)
// cash or deferred arrangements among the excluded items.

const { formChecker, onFile } = require("./formUtil");

